        "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}+1?$",
        "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,-2),(1,0),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,0)}=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=(1)^{2}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/e9cff55c91191b4fa5542d481adf20e29ee55d924da38573ca49ad392fb611bf.png)",
